  <div class="pre-header">WE ARE HOLLISTER</div>
  <h1>The Choices Facing Hollister</h1>
  <div class="subtitle">Staffing. Services. Spending. Accountability.</div>
  <p class="header-note">Hollister is deciding how many positions it can sustain, how public services are structured, and how budget pressures are managed. Those choices involve both the City Manager and the City Council, and they are bigger than any one individual.</p>
</header>

<main class="layout-wrapper">
  <section class="card">
    <h2>What is at stake?</h2>
    <p>The central issue is whether Hollister can reduce its long-term workforce while maintaining service levels, safety, response times and the institutional knowledge residents rely on—and whether the City clearly explains the costs and tradeoffs behind those choices.</p>
    <div class="facts">
      <article class="fact">
        <span class="fact-number">201 → 174</span>
        <span class="fact-label">Long-term staffing target</span>
        <p>In June 2026, the City Manager said the long-term goal was to reduce the City's 201 positions to 174. The adopted 2026–27 budget initially avoided immediate layoffs while voluntary retirement and resignation incentives were considered.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="fact">
        <span class="fact-number">$936K</span>
        <span class="fact-label">Reorganization savings estimate</span>
        <p>In April 2026, the City Council approved a Community Development reorganization that eliminated three positions. The City Manager estimated about $936,000 in annual savings.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="fact">
        <span class="fact-number">$17.1M</span>
        <span class="fact-label">Projected deficits discussed in June</span>
        <p>The June budget discussion identified projected general- and water-fund deficits totaling $17.1 million. Council initially directed borrowing from the sewer fund, but that approach was abandoned in September after repayment constraints were identified.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="work-heading">Who makes these decisions?</h2>
    <div class="card">
      <p>Hollister's own description of the City Manager says the manager administers City operations, recommends policy alternatives, develops the annual budget and implements policies and the financial plan approved by the City Council. Hollister uses a council-manager form of government, and the Council appoints the City Manager.</p>
      <p>Ana Cortez was unanimously appointed on January 5, 2026 and began serving as City Manager on February 2. The current debate is therefore a question of <strong>shared institutional responsibility</strong>: what management recommends, what the Council approves, how those decisions are implemented, and what residents experience afterward.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="role-strip"><strong>The focus should be on the choices themselves.</strong> Staffing levels, service capacity, spending priorities, contracting decisions and public accountability can be evaluated without turning the entire debate into a judgment about one person.</div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="work-heading">What the public record shows</h2>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Staffing reductions remain part of the long-term strategy</h3>
      <p>The City Manager publicly described a long-term goal of reducing the City's position count from 201 to 174. Later budget reporting said layoffs were not initially proposed for the 2026–27 adopted budget, but workforce reductions remained part of the longer-term discussion.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Service levels are part of the tradeoff</h3>
      <p>During June budget discussions, the City Manager said staffing reductions in water and stormwater would mean a lower level of service. That makes service capacity—not just payroll—the central measure of any restructuring plan.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>The sewer-fund borrowing plan changed</h3>
      <p>In June, Council directed borrowing from the sewer fund to address budget gaps. In September, the City reversed course after determining that the amount under consideration would have to be repaid within the same fiscal year. The change underscores how quickly the City's financial strategy has been evolving.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="context-note">Prior employment history can provide background when evaluating an executive appointment, but Hollister's current staffing, budget and service decisions should be judged on the public record in Hollister.</div>
  </section>

